Title: Contradiction: does the git.md stash ban cover a session's own worktree
Created: 2026-11-01
Author: smelt

--- log ---
2026-11-01T21:30Z smelt created

--- body ---
## Context
Exposed by the sixth smelt (2026-11-01), page
`memory/topics/git-worktree-session-guards.md` open questions.

Claim A: `rules/git.md` § Commands that destroy silently — "Never round-trip
through `git stash` in a shared checkout. The stack is repo-global: on a
clean tree `stash` saves nothing and the `pop` grabs someone else's." The
prohibition is scoped to a *shared* checkout, but the stated rationale (the
stack is repo-global) also applies to a session's own worktree: there the
`pop` can still grab another worktree's stash.

Claim B: `rules/git.md` § Branch and commit — "Worktrees are throwaway",
which could be read as making a stash round-trip in one's own throwaway
worktree safe.

## Question for the author
Does the stash ban cover a session's own worktree (stack is repo-global), or
only genuinely shared checkouts? Smelt picks no side and proposes no rule.
